<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Project DefaultTargets="Build" ToolsVersion="4.0" xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/developer/msbuild/2003">
  <!-- Untracked per-machine game locations; see local.props.example. -->
  <Import Project="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)local.props" Condition="Exists('$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)local.props')" />
  <PropertyGroup Label="UserMacros">
    <GAMENAME>HarryPotter</GAMENAME>
    <GAMEDIR>$(GAMEDIR_HARRYPOTTER)</GAMEDIR>
    <!--
      Left empty deliberately. The SDK tree ships an HP.ico and a Launch project but names no built
      executable, and the retail name has not been checked against an installation - so there is no
      expectation to record. deploy.ps1 skips its destination check for a game with no entry, which
      is the safe way to be wrong: a wrong name would refuse a correct install.
    -->
    <GAMEEXE></GAMEEXE>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <PropertyGroup>
    <_ProjectFileVersion>10.0.30319.1</_ProjectFileVersion>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <ItemDefinitionGroup>
    <ClCompile>
      <!--
        Engine 433. Unreal Tournament generation in every respect that usually matters - appSeconds
        returns FTime, GUglyHackFlags and DetailTextures are both present, DECLARE_ABSTRACT_CLASS
        takes the four-argument form, UTexture::Lock takes the FTime overload, and FSceneNode,
        FTextureInfo, FSurfaceInfo, FSurfaceFacet and FTransTexture are identical to UT's.

        What it does not share with UT is the submission interface: KnowWonder replaced the polygon
        fan entry point with indexed triangles, adding the MaxVertices and DrawTriangles pure
        virtuals and demoting DrawGouraudPolygon to a non-pure default body. Their own drivers in
        the SDK implement DrawTriangles and no gouraud entry point at all, so it carries every
        actor. That is what earns this game a define of its own, where XComEnforcer simply reuses
        UNREALTOURNAMENT; the UT-generation sites widen to include it instead.

        Note that this SDK does not compile clean under a conforming compiler. UnAudio.h declares a
        member with no return type, suppressed below, and two headers reuse a for initialiser
        variable after its loop, which is patched in the tree itself - /Zc:forScope- would have
        restored the old behaviour but makes this toolchain fault with an internal compiler error.
      -->
      <PreprocessorDefinitions>HARRYPOTTER;%(PreprocessorDefinitions)</PreprocessorDefinitions>
      <DisableSpecificWarnings>4430;4183;%(DisableSpecificWarnings)</DisableSpecificWarnings>
    </ClCompile>
  </ItemDefinitionGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <BuildMacro Include="GAMENAME">
      <Value>$(GAMENAME)</Value>
    </BuildMacro>
    <BuildMacro Include="GAMEDIR">
      <Value>$(GAMEDIR)</Value>
    </BuildMacro>
    <BuildMacro Include="GAMEEXE">
      <Value>$(GAMEEXE)</Value>
    </BuildMacro>
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
